course: space
lesson: 30
title: What comes next: the Moon, Mars and beyond
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: After fifty years away, people are heading back towards the Moon: it was reported that in April 2026 NASA's Artemis II carried four astronauts around the Moon and back.
explain: Much of what comes next is plans, not facts yet: space missions are hugely hard and costly, and their dates very often slip, like a building project that always finishes late.
explain: The next targets are the Moon's south pole, where dark craters hold water ice, and one day Mars, a trip of many months each way.
explain: Robots go first: spacecraft are on their way to Jupiter's icy moons, and a flying drone is planned for Saturn's moon Titan.
explain: Space near Earth is getting crowded with old satellites and rocket parts, so a tidy-up is part of the future too.
fact: NASA's Artemis programme aims to return people to the Moon.
fact: It was reported that in April 2026 Artemis II carried four astronauts, three American and one Canadian, around the Moon and back, without landing.
fact: The next crewed landing on the Moon is planned for the late 2020s, but dates for big space missions very often slip.
fact: Future missions aim for the Moon's south pole, where some craters are always in shadow and hold water ice, which crews could melt for drinking water and split into oxygen to breathe and rocket fuel.
fact: China's Chang'e 6 brought the first rock samples from the Moon's far side back to Earth in 2024, and China plans to land astronauts on the Moon before 2030.
fact: A trip to Mars takes about six to nine months each way, and good launch chances come only about every 26 months, when Earth and Mars line up.
fact: No humans have yet gone to Mars, and a crewed landing is unlikely before the 2030s, whatever some headlines say.
fact: Perseverance has sealed rock samples in tubes on Mars, but the plan to bring them back to Earth has been delayed and redesigned because of cost.
fact: NASA's Europa Clipper, launched in 2024, should reach Jupiter in 2030, and ESA's JUICE, launched in 2023, should arrive in 2031, to study moons with hidden oceans.
fact: NASA's Dragonfly, a drone-like rotorcraft due to launch around 2028, is planned to fly in the thick air of Saturn's moon Titan in the 2030s.
fact: The International Space Station is planned to be retired and guided down into a remote part of the Pacific around 2030, with new private stations meant to follow.
fact: Thousands of old satellites and pieces of rocket orbit Earth as space junk, and even a paint fleck at orbital speed can damage a spacecraft.
exercise: What does NASA's Artemis programme aim to do? | Return people to the Moon | Send people to Jupiter | Build a telescope on Mars | Close the space station tomorrow
exercise: What was reported about Artemis II in April 2026? | It carried four astronauts around the Moon and back | It landed four astronauts down on the Moon's south pole | It flew four astronauts to Mars | It launched with no crew at all
exercise: Who was on board Artemis II, as reported? | Three American astronauts and one Canadian | Four Chinese astronauts on their first Moon trip | Two British and two Australian astronauts | Twelve American astronauts
exercise: When is the next crewed Moon landing planned? | The late 2020s, though dates often slip | It happened in 2026 for certain | Not before the year 2100 | It is planned for next week
exercise: Why do future missions aim for the Moon's south pole? | Some craters there are always in shadow and hold water ice | It is the warmest and sunniest part of the whole Moon all year | It faces Mars all the time | It has air to breathe
exercise: What did China's Chang'e 6 bring back in 2024? | The first rock samples from the Moon's far side | The first people back from the Moon | Ice from Mars | A piece of the Sun
exercise: When does China plan to land astronauts on the Moon? | Before 2030 | In 1969 | It already did in 2024 | Not before 2100
exercise: How long does a trip to Mars take each way? | About six to nine months | About three days | About ten years | About two weeks
exercise: How often do good launch chances to Mars come? | About every 26 months | Every single day | Once every 100 years | Every 4 weeks
exercise: Have humans been to Mars yet? | No, and a landing is unlikely before the 2030s | Yes, in 2021 with Perseverance | Yes, in 1997 | No, and it is impossible for ever
exercise: What has happened to the plan to bring Perseverance's samples to Earth? | It has been delayed and redesigned because of cost | The samples arrived safely on Earth in 2024 by parachute | The samples were lost in a dust storm | It was finished by astronauts in 2025
exercise: Where are Europa Clipper and JUICE going? | To Jupiter and its icy moons | To Saturn's rings | To the Sun | To the Moon's far side
exercise: What is Dragonfly planned to do? | Fly in the thick air of Saturn's moon Titan | Land the first people on Mars in the late 2020s | Clean up old space junk from around the Earth | Bring back fresh samples of rock from the Moon
exercise: What is planned for the International Space Station around 2030? | To be guided down into a remote part of the Pacific | To be moved away from the Earth to orbit the Moon instead | To be turned into a hotel on Mars | To stay in orbit for ever
exercise: Why is space junk dangerous? | Even a paint fleck at orbital speed can damage a spacecraft | It makes the sky darker at night | It falls as hailstones on big cities nearly every single day | It blocks all radio signals
test: A headline says "Humans will walk on Mars in 2027!". What is the most sensible view? | A crewed landing is unlikely before the 2030s | It is certain, the date is fixed and the rocket is ready | People already live there | It can never happen at all
test: Artemis II went round the Moon. Why did its astronauts not walk on it? | Artemis II was planned to fly round the Moon, not to land | The Moon was much too hot that day for anyone to step out | They forgot to pack their spacesuits before the launch day | Walking on the Moon is now banned by an agreement between countries
test: If a good chance to launch to Mars is missed, about how long until the next good one? | About 26 months | About 26 days | About 26 years | The very next morning
test: Why would water ice on the Moon be so useful to future crews? | It could give drinking water, air and rocket fuel | It could be melted and sold as ice cream to tourists | It keeps the Moon from spinning too fast and wobbling | It makes the Moon's gravity stronger so people don't float
test: lol why can't they just bring the Mars rocks home already | The return plan costs a lot and has been delayed and redesigned | The rocks are far too heavy for any rocket to lift off the ground of Mars | The rocks would melt on Earth | Nobody collected any rocks
test: Which is true about the Moon's far side? | China brought back its first rock samples in 2024 | It is the side that always faces the Earth | Nobody has ever sent anything there | It is always dark and never gets any sunlight at all
test: In a cartoon, the old space station drifts down and crashes into a city. What is the real plan? | Guide it down into a remote part of the Pacific | Let it fall down anywhere at all, just by chance | Push it right out of the solar system with a rocket | Leave it up there for ever as a museum in the sky
test: Your friend says Europa Clipper is already at Jupiter. What is right? | It launched in 2024 and should arrive in 2030 | It landed on Europa in 2023 and found a hidden ocean | It never launched at all | It got there in 2024 within a month
test: Why are scientists excited about Jupiter's and Saturn's icy moons? | Hidden oceans there might be places for life | They are made of solid gold under the ice crust | People could breathe the air on them without suits | They are the nearest moons to Earth after our own
test: If a spacecraft hit a fleck of paint while orbiting at high speed, what could happen? | It could be damaged | Nothing, paint is too soft | It would bounce off safely | It would speed up
test: A rocket launch is pushed back two years. Is this unusual for big space missions? | No, dates for big missions very often slip | Yes, space missions always launch on the planned day | Yes, it has never happened before in the whole space age | No, big missions are almost always early, never late
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reported Artemis II mission of April 2026? | It carried four astronauts around the Moon and back without landing | It landed four astronauts at the lunar south pole | It conveyed a crew to the planet Mars | It was an uncrewed test of a lunar lander upon the surface of the Moon
exam: Ascertain the approximate duration of a journey from the Earth to Mars in one direction. | About six to nine months | About three days | About five years | About two weeks
exam: Identify the principal reason for which future lunar missions are directed towards the south pole of the Moon. | Permanently shadowed craters there contain water ice | That region has a breathable atmosphere | That region is permanently in sunlight and is therefore warm | That region faces the planet Mars
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established plans concerning the International Space Station. | It is to be retired and guided into a remote part of the Pacific around 2030 | It is to be relocated to an orbit around the Moon to serve the Artemis missions | It is to remain in orbit indefinitely | It was retired and brought down in 2011
